# Normalise line endings to LF in the repository, and check them out as LF.
#
# This matters more than usual here for three reasons:
#
#   - The Docker build copies these files into a Linux image, so a working
#     tree with CRLF would build V sources with CRLF.
#   - Code examples are embedded byte for byte into the binary, and the editor
#     marks the line the compiler complained about. Keeping the bytes stable
#     between what is committed and what is served is the point.
#   - Windows and Linux contributors should produce the same commit.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets, never touched.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary

# V sources and templates: LF, and treated as text so git will not try to be
# clever about them.
*.v text eol=lf
*.vsh text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
.gitignore text eol=lf
